schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Retroactive funding pays for demonstrated impact, not promises"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Retroactive funding (paying for work after it's done and its impact is
    demonstrated, rather than funding promises up front) has become a core
    Web3 funding pattern — popularized by Optimism's RetroPGF rounds and
    used in Gitcoin Grants 24 (GG24, late 2025 into 2026), which deployed
    six funding mechanisms at once including retroactive funding and
    peer-reviewed hypercerts.
  evidence: >-
    Article states the RetroPGF logic ("easier to agree on what was useful
    than to predict what will be") and cites GG24's six-mechanism design as
    a concrete instance, without linking a primary GG24 results report.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/why-measurement-matters.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used to argue that for a Web3-native local node, measurement is the
    funding rail itself, not paperwork filed after funding — "no record, no
    reward" is the article's summary line.
  uncertainty: >-
    GG24 mechanism count and framing taken from the article's summary; no
    direct citation to Gitcoin's own GG24 results/retrospective was checked
    here, and "core pattern" is the article's characterization rather than
    a cited adoption metric.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/why-measurement-matters.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
